174	## PT3. Types and check domains
175	
176	- **PT3.1** The types are:
177	  - `bool`;
178	  - enums;
179	  - integer ranges `a..b`;
180	  - `int`;
181	  - `string`;
182	  - `map<K, T>`, where the key type `K` is an enum or an integer range `0..n`. A map keyed by an enum lowers to a JSON object keyed by constructor name. A map keyed by `0..n` lowers to a JSON array of length `n + 1`. A range key that does not start at 0 is `PL1602 ARRAY_MAP_NOT_ZERO_BASED`. So a corpus field such as `taskStatuses: ["QUEUED", "RUNNING", "FAILED"]` is `map<0..2, Status>`;
183	  - **tagged unions** `type U = C1 { … } | … | Cn { … }` (PS1.4), finite when their payload fields are. Handlers read a union with `match` or `is` and write it as a whole value. The lowering flattens it to a tag field and nullable payload fields, with an automatic `shape:` invariant (PS9.1b). Below the top of a field (a map's value, a payload, a parameter) it lowers to one JSON object `{ "$": "C", p: v, … }` with exactly its constructor's payloads, each lowered by the same rule; a union with no payload stays its constructor's name (S65). An effect parameter or a `childState` field of such a type is `PL0901`. Constructors of one union may share a payload name only with the same type, and then share one flat field; the same name with two types is `PL1601 PAYLOAD_TYPE_CONFLICT`.
184	  - **records** `type R = { f1: T1, …, fn: Tn }` (S25): a nominal type whose values have every field and no tag. A record is finite when its fields are; it has no optional fields and may not refer to itself, directly or through other types. It lowers to a plain JSON object and compares field by field. A record literal `{ f1: e1, … }` lists every field (`PL1502`). A literal `{ k: v, … }` is a map or a record according to its expected type, as S22 resolves constructors; with no expected type it is `PL1005`. A record may be used wherever a type may: as a field, a parameter, a payload, or a map value. `r.f` reads field `f` of a record value.
185	- **PT3.2** Every field and parameter has a finite check domain (PF1.2). Enums, `bool`, ranges and maps over finite value types are finite by construction. `int` and `string` need an explicit `check` clause. Without one, the compiler reports `PL1101 UNBOUNDED_DOMAIN`.
186	- **PT3.3** A `check` clause narrower than the runtime type is an assumption. It is written to `dataDomain` and to the certificate's `domains`, and it is graded by `poly grade` (polynv).
187	- **PT3.4** Each `check` clause has a mode, and the mode decides what the runtime does with a value outside the check domain:
188	
189	  | Mode | Runtime behaviour | Certificate |
190	  |---|---|---|
191	  | `strict` | `dispatch` rejects the action with the reason `outside-check-domain` before the handler runs. | The checked domain is the running domain. |
192	  | `disclosed` | The action is accepted and journaled with the flag `outsideCheckDomain`. `poly export traces` surfaces these windows as domain-drift evidence for polynv. | The field is listed under `disclosedDomains`. |
193	
194	  An explicit `strict` on a parameter of an action with no handler is `PL1302 STRICT_ON_UNHANDLED_ACTION`, at the declaration: such an action is not in the lowered module (S35), so the runtime answers it `unhandled` and never runs the check. The fixes are to remove `strict` or to add a handler (S53).
195	
196	- **PT3.4a** The default mode is `strict` for every parameter and field whose value can flow into the payload of an `irreversible` or `compensable` effect. The compiler computes this flow from the handlers. It is `disclosed` elsewhere. An explicit `disclosed` on a field that flows into an irreversible effect is `PL1301 DISCLOSED_IRREVERSIBLE_INPUT`, `PL1302 STRICT_ON_UNHANDLED_ACTION`, and a person must accept it (PT12.5).
197	- **PT3.5** An assignment that can produce a value outside a field's declared type (for example `items' = items + 1` with `items: 0..3`) is not a compile error. It lowers to an automatic state invariant, `domain:<field>`. The model check then reports the shortest path that overflows (`PL3102 DOMAIN_ESCAPE`) with a witness.
198	
199	## PT4. Handlers and step semantics
200	
201	### PT4.1 Handler shape and evaluation order
202	
203	Each action has at most one handler. A handler lowers to one keyed acceptor in the SAM v2 strict profile. A handler body runs in two phases:
204	
205	1. **Guard phase.** Every `require` evaluates against the pre-state and the parameters.
206	   - The rule is per path. On every control path from the handler entry, every `require` on that path comes before the first write on that path. A `require` inside an `if` branch is legal when no write precedes it on that branch or before the `if`. A violation is `PL2101 WRITE_BEFORE_REQUIRE`, with the offending path.
207	   - A failing `require` calls `reject(reason)` before any write.
208	   - A `reject "reason"` statement (used, for example, in a `match` arm) rejects unconditionally. It is subject to the same rule: a `reject` after a write on the same path is `PL2101`.
209	   - Agent-written code therefore cannot raise the `rejectAfterWrite` frame error.
210	2. **Write phase.** Primed assignments write to `next`. `emit`, `after`, `cancel`, `spawn` and `signal` statements are collected as effect intents (PT6).
211	
212	### PT4.2 Frame derivation
213	
214	The compiler computes the set of fields written on each path. It emits `unchanged(...)` for the complement, on that path. The author never writes a frame.
215	
216	### PT4.3 Cross-cutting vetoes
217	
218	`guard all when <expr> reject "<reason>"` lowers to a broadcast acceptor. The profile allows it: one acceptor may reject after another has written. Guards run before handlers. A guard reads the pre-state and `action`; it reads an action's parameters only where `action == A` narrows the action, under the name resolution of PT2.2 (S19).
219	
220	### PT4.4 Step classification
221	
222	A step is:
223	
224	- `accepted` when at least one field is written or an effect is emitted;
225	- `rejected` when a `require` or guard fails;
226	- `unhandled` when the action has no handler.
227	
228	A handler that can complete with no write, no effect and no rejection is reported as `PL2201 SILENT_NOOP`. This follows the doctrine "every no-op is observable".
229	
230	### PT4.4a Terminal states
231	
232	`terminal <expr>` is a predicate over the state. The compiler requires it to depend only on the primary control field, which is the first field of the `state` block (the polygraph contract rule "the first key is treated as the primary control state"). It lowers to `terminalStates`, the list of values of that field that satisfy the predicate. A terminal predicate that reads any other field is `PL1401 TERMINAL_NOT_ON_CONTROL_FIELD`.
233	
234	## PT7. Composition
235	
236	A machine that spawns children is checked with its children as a product:
237	
238	- Each cascade is atomic.
239	- Per-instance dispatch has a single writer.
240	- `MAX_CASCADE_DEPTH` is 8.
241	
242	These rules are polyrun's composition semantics, unchanged, and they apply to **attached** children only. The compiler emits `invariants.compose.mjs` from `compose invariant` declarations. Joint state is addressed as `parent.<field>` and `children.<key>.<field>`. The runtime places an attached tree on the shard of its root and refuses to place a descendant elsewhere (`PL5202 ATTACHED_ACROSS_SHARDS`, PS7.4).
243	
244	A compose invariant reads `children.k.f` only under a `k spawned` narrowing, as `action == A` narrows parameters (S19): `k spawned implies children.k.shipState == Delivered`. A read without it is `PL5103 CHILD_READ_WITHOUT_SPAWNED`, and the fix is the narrowing (S48). A family is reached through `forall` and the aggregates: `forall i in f: f[i] spawned implies children.f[i].ship != Pending`, and `count(children.f[*].ship == Delivered)`, where an aggregate counts only the spawned members (S49).
245	
246	polyrun's built-in doctrine findings are surfaced as poly diagnostics (S47):
247	
248	- child-key collision: `PL5101 CHILDKEY_COLLISION`;
249	- a cascade delivery (completion, cancel or signal) that reaches an action with no handler: `PL5102 UNHANDLED_CASCADE_DELIVERY`;
250	- a signal to a child that is not spawned, on which the runtime halts the parent: `PL5104 SIGNAL_TO_ABSENT_CHILD`, a finding with its path (S50);
251	- an unnamed reject, or the poison class: `PL2501 LOWERING_MISMATCH`, since every poly reject has a reason and only a lowering that lost something can reach either.
252	
253	## PT7a. Configurations
254	
255	A configuration (PS13) is the unit of checking for machines that talk to each other. It has no engine artefact. poly-ref explores it, as it explores liveness.
256	
257	```poly
258	module checkout
259	
260	service machine Inventory {
261	  state { onHand: 0..1 = 1 }
262	  action Query(from: Ref<Order>)
263	  action Take(from: Ref<Order>)
264	
265	  on Query(from) { send from <- Available(n: onHand) }
266	  on Take(from) {
267	    if onHand > 0 { onHand' = onHand - 1   send from <- Taken() }
268	    else          { send from <- OutOfStock() }
269	  }
270	}
271	
272	type OrderState = Idle | Asked | Taking | Done | Refused
273	
274	machine Order {
275	  state { st: OrderState = Idle   inv: Ref<Inventory> = none }
276	  terminal st is Done | Refused
277	  action Place()  action Available(n: 0..1)  action Taken()  action OutOfStock()
278	
279	  on Place      { require st is Idle   else reject "not-idle"    st' = Asked   send inv <- Query(from: self) }
280	  on Available(n) { require st is Asked else reject "not-asked"
281	                    if n > 0 { st' = Taking   send inv <- Take(from: self) } else { st' = Refused } }
282	  // Taking waits for the inventory's answer before the order is confirmed (Done)
283	  on Taken      { require st is Taking else reject "not-taking"  st' = Done }
284	  on OutOfStock { require st is Taking else reject "not-taking"  st' = Refused }
285	}
286	
287	configuration two-buyers {
288	  inv       : Inventory
289	  order[2]  : Order { inv: inv }
290	  inputs    { order[*].Place }
291	  bounds    { transit 4 }
292	  invariant confirmed-within-stock: count(order[*] is Done) <= init(inv.onHand)
293	  liveness  settle: forall i in order: order[i] is Asked leads to order[i] is Done | Refused
294	}
295	```
296	
297	Both machines are safe on their own: the inventory never lets `onHand` go below zero, and each order's handlers respect its own states. Now make the order **optimistic**. On `Available(n)` with `n > 0`, it confirms at once (`st' = Done`) and sends `Take` afterwards, instead of waiting in `Taking`. That is the classic check-then-act race between actors. Every message is handled correctly by its receiver, the per-machine checks stay clean, and the interleaving confirms two orders against one unit of stock:
298	
299	```
300	$ poly check checkout.poly
301	checkout.poly:33  PL5401 CONFIGURATION_INVARIANT  confirmed-within-stock
302	  witness (6 global steps):
303	    input   Place@order[0]
304	    input   Place@order[1]
305	    deliver Query(order[0])   -> inv
306	    deliver Query(order[1])   -> inv          both queries answered with onHand = 1
307	    deliver Available(1)      -> order[0]     order[0] confirmed
308	    deliver Available(1)      -> order[1]     order[1] confirmed; one unit, two confirmations
309	verdict: FINDINGS (exit 1)  over 2 orders, 1 inventory, transit ≤ 4
310	```
311	
312	With the order waiting for `Taken`, the configuration is clean over 44 global states within the same bounds. No quiescent state is stuck, and every placed order settles. An independent exploration of both versions is in [`reviews/evidence/config_check.py`](reviews/evidence/config_check.py). No per-machine check can find this defect. It lives in the arrival order and in an invariant that spans instances, and neither machine sees both.
313	
314	**Lowering and runtime.**
315	
316	- A `send` lowers to a new built-in intent kind, `sendTo { target, action, data }`, beside polyrun's `spawnChild` and `signalChild`. This is a small change to polyrun (architecture §2a). An address is the target instance's id, and `none` is `null` (S58).
317	- A detached child's address is resolved at the commit of the step that creates the child, not at delivery: resolving against the sender would be wrong once the parent passes the address to a third machine (S78).
318	- `self` is legal only as a whole argument of a `send`. It lowers to the marker `{ "$ref": "self" }`, which the kernel replaces with the sender's instance id when it writes the message, since neither the acceptor nor the mapper knows that id (S58). `send … as n` is `PL0901`.
319	- The runtime delivers it through the outbox, at least once, as a `dispatch` to the target with the intent's idempotency key as its `actionId`. So each message takes exactly one step at its receiver (K4, PS14.3). The outbox row is keyed `<sender>:<seq>:<position>`, PS13.2's message identity, and the receiver's dedupe on it for the life of the instance is K4. There is no fixed delivery order: unordered is the default (PT9.8), and `fifo` needs the in-order worker. A send to an instance that does not exist goes to the dead-letter list and is journaled on the sender's run. Tests and the M3 adapter may deliver one message at a time (S89).
320	- A delivery the receiver rejects is a rejected step there. The sender is not notified unless the receiver sends a reply, which is the actor discipline.
321	- `fifo` between a pair of instances is available only when they share a kernel shard, and it is checked with the narrower arrival order.
322	
323	**Names in configuration properties (S32).** In a configuration property, `x.f` and `g[i].f` name field `f` of instance `x` or `g[i]`. `g[i] is C` and `g[*] is C` resolve `C` against the machine's fields: the test is legal when exactly one field's type has the constructor `C`, and it then tests that field; otherwise it is `PL1005 AMBIGUOUS_CONSTRUCTOR`, whose fix is `g[i].f is C`. So `count(order[*] is Done)` tests `st`, the one field of `Order` whose type has `Done`, and `init(inv.onHand)` is the inventory's initial stock. A field access on an instance is legal only in configuration properties, never inside a machine (PS1.6).
324	
325	**A send to `none` (S29).** Inside a configuration, a step that sends to `none` is `PL5404 SEND_TO_NONE`, a finding with the path to the step; the message is not added to `μ`. In a single-machine check, the send is an ordinary intent (PS4.4).
326	
327	**Configurations in the pipeline.** Stage 4b of the check pipeline (architecture §5) explores every declared configuration. Every verdict carries the configuration's bounds (PT12.3a). A bound hit is `INCONCLUSIVE` (`PL5301`, `PL5302`), never `CLEAN`. A configuration without `bounds { transit n }` is `PL5303 CONFIGURATION_WITHOUT_BOUNDS` (S62): the program may type-check perfectly, and without the bound the exploration need not end. `instances n` is required only when a machine of the configuration spawns detached children, and reaching it is `PL5302` (S60). A configuration whose machine spawns attached children is `PL0901` until a later milestone (S61).
328	
329	**Liveness in a configuration (S56).** A liveness property may begin with `forall i in g:`, which scopes over the whole `leads to` (PS13.6), and may leave out `under fair { … }`: delivery is fair by PS14.2, and an input is assumed fair only when the clause names it. In a machine the clause stays required.
330	
331	## PT8. Policies
332	// The combinators of poly's embedded surface (PT2.5, S71), as `tsc` checks an embedded module against them.
333	// They are declarations only: poly-embed reads the module's syntax with the TypeScript compiler API and never
334	// runs it. A name inside an arrow is a property of its parameter, `$.x`, so `$` is untyped here; the poly
335	// checker types the program after reading it, as it types the standalone syntax.
336	
337	declare const node: unique symbol;
338	interface Node<K extends string> { readonly [node]: K }
339	
340	export type Decl = Node<'decl'>;
341	export type Item = Node<'item'>;
342	export type Stmt = Node<'stmt'>;
343	export type Arm = Node<'arm'>;
344	export type Pattern = Node<'pattern'>;
